# 🔧 Feature Engineering

Two important features were created during the machine learning workflow.

## Product Category

Products were classified into:

* `Perishable`
* `Non-Perishable`

The following product types were considered perishable:

```text
Dairy
Meat
Fruits and Vegetables
Breakfast
Breads
Seafood
```

The logic is:

```python
perishable_items = [
    "Dairy",
    "Meat",
    "Fruits and Vegetables",
    "Breakfast",
    "Breads",
    "Seafood"
]

data["Product_Category"] = np.where(
    data["Product_Type"].isin(perishable_items),
    "Perishable",
    "Non-Perishable"
)
```

---

## Product Family

The first two characters of `Product_Id` were extracted to create:

```text
Product_Family
```

For example:

```text
FD12345 → FD
DR12345 → DR
NC12345 → NC
```

This resulted in the following product family categories:

```text
FD
DR
NC
```

---

# 🗑️ Dropped Features

The following original columns were removed after feature engineering:

```text
Product_Id
Store_Id
Product_Type
Store_Establishment_Year
```

### Reason

`Product_Id` was used to create `Product_Family`.

`Product_Type` was used to create `Product_Category`.

`Store_Establishment_Year` was transformed into:

```text
Store_Age_Years
```

`Store_Id` was considered an identifier rather than a useful predictive feature.

---

# 📊 Model Features

The final model uses the following features:

```text
Product_Weight
Product_Sugar_Content
Product_Allocated_Area
Product_MRP
Store_Size
Store_Location_City_Type
Store_Type
Store_Age_Years
Product_Category
Product_Family
```

---

# ⚙️ Data Preprocessing

The final model is implemented as a Scikit-learn Pipeline.

## Numerical Features

The following features are standardized using `StandardScaler`:

```text
Product_Weight
Product_Allocated_Area
Product_MRP
Store_Age_Years
```

## Categorical Features

The following features are encoded using `OneHotEncoder`:

```text
Product_Sugar_Content
Store_Size
Store_Location_City_Type
Store_Type
Product_Category
Product_Family
```

The encoder uses:

```python
OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore")
```

This allows the API to safely handle previously unseen categorical values.

---

# 🤖 Final Machine Learning Model

Multiple machine learning models were evaluated during the project.

The final selected model was:

```text
RandomForestRegressor
```

The optimized Random Forest model achieved approximately:

```text
R² Score: 0.9317
```

on the unseen test data.

The model demonstrated strong predictive performance and was selected as the final production model.

---

# 💾 Model Serialization

The trained pipeline was serialized using `joblib`.

Model file:

```text
backend/superkart_sales_pipeline.joblib
```

The serialized object contains the complete machine learning pipeline:

```text
Input Data
    │
    ▼
ColumnTransformer
    │
    ├── StandardScaler
    │
    └── OneHotEncoder
    │
    ▼
RandomForestRegressor
    │
    ▼
Prediction
```

This means the API does not need to manually reproduce the scaling and encoding logic.

---

# 🔌 REST API

The backend is implemented using Flask.

## Backend URL

[https://superkart-sales-forecasting.onrender.com](https://superkart-sales-forecasting.onrender.com)

---

## Health Check

### Endpoint

```text
GET /health
```

Example:

```bash
curl https://superkart-sales-forecasting.onrender.com/health
```

Expected response:

```json
{
  "model_loaded": true,
  "service": "SuperKart Sales Prediction API",
  "status": "healthy"
}
```

---

# 🔮 Prediction API

### Endpoint

```text
POST /v1/predict
```

### Content Type

```text
application/json
```

---

## Request Example

```bash
curl -X POST https://superkart-sales-forecasting.onrender.com/v1/predict \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "Product_Weight": 9.3,
    "Product_Sugar_Content": "Low Sugar",
    "Product_Allocated_Area": 0.016,
    "Product_MRP": 249.8,
    "Store_Size": "Medium",
    "Store_Location_City_Type": "Tier 2",
    "Store_Type": "Supermarket Type1",
    "Store_Age_Years": 10,
    "Product_Category": "Perishable",
    "Product_Family": "FD"
  }'
```

Example response:

```json
{
  "predicted_sales": 3646.94,
  "status": "success"
}
```

---

# 📥 API Input Features

The prediction endpoint expects:

| Feature                  | Type    | Example           |
| ------------------------ | ------- | ----------------- |
| Product_Weight           | Float   | 9.3               |
| Product_Sugar_Content    | String  | Low Sugar         |
| Product_Allocated_Area   | Float   | 0.016             |
| Product_MRP              | Float   | 249.8             |
| Store_Size               | String  | Medium            |
| Store_Location_City_Type | String  | Tier 2            |
| Store_Type               | String  | Supermarket Type1 |
| Store_Age_Years          | Integer | 10                |
| Product_Category         | String  | Perishable        |
| Product_Family           | String  | FD                |

---

# 🖥️ Streamlit Frontend

The frontend is built using Streamlit.

It provides an interactive interface where users can enter:

### Product Information

* Product Weight
* Product Sugar Content
* Product Allocated Area
* Product MRP
* Product Category
* Product Family

### Store Information

* Store Size
* Store Location City Type
* Store Type
* Store Age

The frontend sends the input data to the Flask API and displays the predicted sales value.

---

# 🌐 Frontend URL

The deployed application is available at:

[https://superkart-sales-forecasting-1.onrender.com](https://superkart-sales-forecasting-1.onrender.com)

Users can access this URL directly from a web browser.

---

# 🔗 Frontend ↔ Backend Configuration

The frontend does not hard-code the production backend URL.

Instead, it uses an environment variable:

```text
BACKEND_URL
```

The Streamlit application contains:

```python
BACKEND_URL = os.getenv(
    "BACKEND_URL",
    "http://localhost:7860"
).rstrip("/")

PREDICTION_URL = f"{BACKEND_URL}/v1/predict"
```

### Local Environment

```text
BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:7860
```

### Production Environment

```text
BACKEND_URL=https://superkart-sales-forecasting.onrender.com
```

This allows the same frontend code to work both locally and in production.

---

# 🐳 Docker

Both the backend and frontend are containerized using Docker.

## Backend Dockerfile

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.11-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip && \
    pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY sales_prediction_api.py .
COPY superkart_sales_pipeline.joblib .

EXPOSE 10000

CMD ["sh", "-c", "gunicorn --workers 2 --bind 0.0.0.0:${PORT:-10000} sales_prediction_api:app"]
```

---

## Frontend Dockerfile

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.11-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip && \
    pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY app.py .

EXPOSE 10000

CMD ["sh", "-c", "streamlit run app.py --server.address=0.0.0.0 --server.port=${PORT:-10000} --server.headless=true --server.enableCORS=false --server.enableXsrfProtection=false"]
```

---

# 💻 Running the Project Locally

## Prerequisites

Install:

* Python 3.11+
* Docker
* Git

---

# Backend Setup

Navigate to the backend directory:

```bash
cd backend
```

Install dependencies:

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Run the API:

```bash
python sales_prediction_api.py
```

The API will run on:

```text
http://localhost:7860
```

Test the health endpoint:

```bash
curl http://localhost:7860/health
```

---

# Frontend Setup

Open another terminal:

```bash
cd frontend
```

Install dependencies:

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

Set the backend URL:

### Linux/macOS

```bash
export BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:7860
```

### Windows PowerShell

```powershell
$env:BACKEND_URL="http://localhost:7860"
```

Run Streamlit:

```bash
streamlit run app.py
```

The frontend will normally be available at:

```text
http://localhost:8501
```

---

# 🐳 Running with Docker

## Build Backend Image

From the project root:

```bash
docker build -t superkart-sales-backend ./backend
```

Run:

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name superkart-backend \
  -p 7860:7860 \
  superkart-sales-backend
```

---

## Build Frontend Image

```bash
docker build -t superkart-sales-frontend ./frontend
```

Run:

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name superkart-frontend \
  -p 8501:8501 \
  -e BACKEND_URL=http://host.docker.internal:7860 \
  superkart-sales-frontend
```

For Docker-to-Docker communication, a shared Docker network can be used:

```bash
docker network create superkart-network
```

Connect the backend:

```bash
docker network connect superkart-network superkart-backend
```

Run the frontend:

```bash
docker run -d \
  --name superkart-frontend \
  --network superkart-network \
  -p 8501:8501 \
  -e BACKEND_URL=http://superkart-backend:7860 \
  superkart-sales-frontend
```

